11.6 Minimum gate for final factorial results
Every endpoint in the running mixed-level loss study must preserve:

source file/hash and provenance version;
units, sampling rate, length, and lead order;
eligible/excluded record counts with reasons;
per-record outputs for paired analysis;
common record-order hashes across loss cells;
uncertainty clustered at the patient/record level;
label status: adjudicated, machine derived, inferred, or unknown;
result status: complete, partial, failed, or placeholder.

12.6 Generation-bound evaluation protocol
A defensible external delineation result must bind each row to the exact model generation. For every checkpoint, the evaluation artifact should record:

model ID, mask, seed, checkpoint SHA-256, source-bundle SHA-256, training contract, and checkpoint byte count;
a content digest for the Zhejiang signal and label inputs, plus documented units and acquisition sampling rate;
the input-lead mapping and all preprocessing, including any resampling and annotation-index transform;
per-record and per-wave Dice, onset error, offset error, detection failures, and paired-record coverage—not only an aggregate mean;
paired uncertainty across records and multiplicity control for the predeclared factorial contrasts;
a failure ledger for missing waves, unusable records, and numerical errors.

The storage layer described later in the book already supports the first requirement: an archived checkpoint is materialized by exact content hash for inference and removed from the local cache afterward. The current Zhejiang evaluator does not yet satisfy the remaining provenance and coverage requirements and its one-row output must remain a pipeline check.

13.7 Provenance and reconstruction boundary
The live tensor audit in the opening chapter pairs tensors back to raw 500 Hz WFDB records and verifies split membership, shapes, finite values, content roots, and sample agreement. The authoritative release metadata and local contract—not this plotting code—define units and sample rate. A downstream evaluator must reject a model when any of the following differ from its sidecar:

lead order or observed indices;
sampling rate, duration, units, scaling, filtering, or normalization;
split record/patient membership or byte-content roots;
padding/cropping and acquired-lead pass-through behavior;
source bundle, state schema, or checkpoint digest.

This contract is what makes archived per-model inference meaningful: materializing the exact bytes is necessary, but those bytes are usable only with the exact preprocessing and lead semantics recorded alongside them.

14.6 Architecture comparison rules
The cleanest loss-function estimand is within-family: compare objective masks while holding architecture, seed, split, preprocessing, and evaluator fixed. An architecture-family contrast changes multiple mechanisms and should not be interpreted as a pure effect of convolution, variational inference, or attention.
For every reported model, the minimum identity is:

registry ID and architecture family;
objective mask and seed;
observed-lead order and pass-through policy;
checkpoint digest and source-bundle digest;
preprocessing and split content roots;
adapter/evaluation-code digest;
state schema and precision.

The 480-model study satisfies this identity through its checkpoint sidecars and SQLite/remote-blob catalog. The 48-cell study satisfies it through its locked registry and referenced-artifact bundle. Neither justifies substituting a similarly named architecture from another script or checkpoint generation.

15.7 Energy distance—not signal energy
The ED bit activates empirical energy distance across records, computed separately for each lead. If X_c=\{\hat y_{bc,:}\}_{b=1}^B and Y_c=\{y_{bc,:}\}_{b=1}^B are batches of complete lead waveforms,

\mathcal L_{\mathrm{ED}}=\frac{1}{C}\sum_c\left[
2\,\overline{\|X_c-Y_c\|_2}
-\overline{\|X_c-X'_c\|_2}
-\overline{\|Y_c-Y'_c\|_2}
\right],

where the bars denote means over all ordered batch pairs, including self-pairs in the within-sample terms. It is not |\sum_t\hat y^2-\sum_ty^2| and does not directly constrain physical electrical energy. Because the empirical distribution is the current mini-batch, its value and gradient depend on batch composition and batch size.

21.7 What remains unverified
The retained evidence does not permit this chapter to recompute:

per-model and per-task AUROC/AP/Brier/ECE from record-level probabilities;
paired patient-cluster confidence intervals for reconstruction-versus-reference deltas;
label and probability identity across every archived model file;
subgroup, calibration-tail, or worst-record analyses;
the effect of waveform reconstruction separately from unchanged metadata.

Restoring a compact, deidentified record-level release database would enable those analyses without restoring bulky checkpoints. Until then, this page is an integrity-checked evidence atlas, not an independently executable classifier-validation analysis.

22.3 Model-level evidence contract
For each model, the final table will include:

full model_id, mask, seed, architecture, and observed-lead set;
checkpoint SHA-256, byte size, state schema, and archive locator;
source-bundle, environment, preprocessing, and train/validation/test content roots;
convergence diagnostics and prespecified exclusion status;
paired patient-level reconstruction, morphology, calibration, and downstream-task metrics;
uncertainty intervals and subgroup denominators;
inference recipe proving that the archived model can be materialized and loaded.

22.4 Why the previous model-by-model prose was withdrawn
The previous draft assigned clinical diagnoses such as “strictly preserves T-wave termination” from aggregate feature means in a legacy CSV. That inference was not supported. A mean QT difference cannot establish waveform-level boundary preservation; it also cannot distinguish delineator failure, pairing error, variance collapse, or checkpoint-generation mismatch. The source file lacked the identity fields needed to resolve those alternatives.
The correct diagnostic chain is:

verify checkpoint and dataset identity;
retain paired per-record outputs and delineation quality flags;
report valid-pair denominators and failure rates;
estimate paired bias, limits of agreement, robust slope, and coverage;
stratify by heart rate, rhythm, morphology, sex, age, and signal quality;
inspect representative waveforms under a prespecified selection rule;
only then make a bounded claim about preservation.

24.4 Electrophysiology and interval safety
QT/QTc, PR, QRS duration, axis, Brugada morphology, pre-excitation, and atrial activity require separate analyses because their failure mechanisms differ. Interval validation must report:

the correction formula and heart-rate range for QTc;
paired bias and 95% limits of agreement, not correlation alone;
threshold reclassification around clinically chosen cut points;
delineation failure and abstention rates;
subgroup results for rhythm, conduction abnormality, sex, age, medication, and signal quality;
whether the same beats were measurable in the source and reconstruction.

A model can have high correlation while producing clinically meaningful threshold crossings. Conversely, a few large errors can dominate safety even when average bias is small.

24.9 Minimum release checklist

independent multisite cohort and locked analysis plan;
exact checkpoint, preprocessing, and evaluation digests;
patient-level pairing with no train/test identity leakage;
measured-ECG and reduced-lead baselines;
morphology, diagnostic, calibration, subgroup, and failure-rate reporting;
prespecified margins and multiplicity control;
uncertainty or abstention behavior under poor signal quality;
clinician-readable disclosure that leads are reconstructed;
prospective human-factors and workflow validation before clinical deployment.

This checklist is intentionally demanding. The scientifically useful result may be that reconstruction helps some endpoints, harms others, and should abstain for specific morphologies. A trustworthy benchmark must make those boundaries visible.
